<!-- ============================================================
     DD-WRT CVE-2021-27137: SSDP/UPnP Stack Buffer Overflow
     C0XMO Gafgyt Variant — Multi-Architecture IoT Botnet

     Threat:
       CVE-2021-27137 is a stack-based buffer overflow in the UPnP
       SSDP parser of DD-WRT router firmware versions prior to
       changeset 45724. The vulnerability exists in
       router/upnp/src/ssdp.c where an unsafe strcpy() copies
       user-supplied ST:uuid: header values from SSDP M-SEARCH
       requests into a fixed-size stack buffer without bounds
       checking. An unauthenticated attacker can trigger the overflow
       by sending a crafted UDP M-SEARCH packet to port 1900 with an
       oversized ST:uuid: value, resulting in denial of service or
       arbitrary code execution under the security context of the
       upnpd process.

       CISA KEV: Added July 2026. Federal deadline July 24, 2026.
       Active exploitation attributed to C0XMO, a cross-platform
       Gafgyt-family botnet (FortiGuard Labs, BleepingComputer 2026)
       targeting ARM, MIPS, PowerPC, SuperH, MC68000, and x86_64
       architectures. C0XMO separates scanning from propagation into
       distinct components and kills rival malware processes after
       infection to maintain exclusive control of the device.

       UPnP is disabled by default on DD-WRT and when enabled only
       listens on internal interfaces; exploitation therefore requires
       LAN-side access, making the primary risk a compromised LAN host
       or direct physical access to the internal network.

     Attack chain (C0XMO):
       1. Python scanner from 217.160.125.125:15527 probes LAN for
          hosts with UDP 1900 open (DD-WRT UPnP enabled).
       2. Crafted M-SEARCH packet with oversized ST:uuid: value
          overflows the stack buffer in ssdp_msearch().
       3. Shellcode executes and fetches the C0XMO ELF dropper from
          217.160.125.125:15527 via HTTP GET.
       4. Dropper is written to /tmp/.cache and executed; architecture-
          specific binaries (e.g. mipsel, armv7l) are staged there.
       5. C0XMO kills rival botnet processes (Mirai, Gafgyt variants)
          and initiates DDoS attack campaigns and further SSDP scanning.

     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1190     - Exploit Public-Facing Application
       T1105     - Ingress Tool Transfer (ELF dropper download)
       T1498     - Network Denial of Service (Gafgyt DDoS capability)
       T1496     - Resource Hijacking (botnet enrollment)
       T1071     - Application Layer Protocol (C0XMO C2 over HTTP)
       T1584.005 - Compromise Infrastructure: Botnet

     Rule IDs: 104128-104134
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Issue: ESKA-611
     Date: 2026-07-21

     Log source prerequisites:
       Rules 104128-104129 (inbound SSDP scanning):
         Firewall logs (firewall group) with data.dest_port and
         data.src_ip fields. OPNsense/pfSense syslog forwarded to
         Wazuh covers this via the firewall group decoder.
       Rule 104130 (C0XMO dropper IOC - firewall):
         Firewall logs with data.dest_ip and data.dest_port fields.
         Any outbound connection from a LAN host to 217.160.125.125
         indicates compromise; DD-WRT itself does not run a Wazuh
         agent and will not generate this alert directly.
       Rule 104131 (C0XMO dropper IOC - Suricata):
         Suricata EVE JSON logs forwarded to Wazuh (parent SID 86601).
         Suricata must have network visibility on the LAN segment
         where DD-WRT devices reside to observe exploitation traffic.
       Rule 104132 (endpoint wget/curl to dropper port):
         Wazuh agent on Linux host (command monitoring or auditd).
         Detects the wget/curl process that fetches the C0XMO ELF
         binary from the dropper server on port 15527; relevant if
         a compromised Linux host is being used as the pivot for
         SSDP scanning and dropper delivery.
       Rule 104133 (endpoint /tmp/.cache dropper staging):
         Wazuh agent with syscheck FIM configured to monitor /tmp/.
         Covers the ELF binary drop location used by C0XMO on
         compromised Linux/embedded devices accessible to Wazuh.
       Rule 104134 (internal SSDP scanning from LAN host):
         Firewall logs showing internal-to-internal UDP 1900 traffic.
         Compromised DD-WRT devices and Linux hosts enrolled in
         C0XMO will scan LAN subnets for additional UPnP targets
         using the embedded Python scanner.

     Severity tiers:
       0  - Inbound UDP 1900 from external IP (anchor for scanner rule)
       11 - Inbound UDP 1900 scanner: 5+ probes from same external IP
              within 60 s (automated SSDP reconnaissance)
       14 - Outbound to C0XMO dropper IP 217.160.125.125 (confirmed IOC;
              LAN host contacted dropper server after exploit shellcode ran)
       14 - Suricata: Traffic to/from C0XMO dropper IP 217.160.125.125
       12 - Endpoint: wget/curl outbound to port 15527 (C0XMO dropper port)
       11 - Endpoint: File creation in /tmp/.cache (C0XMO dropper staging)
       12 - Internal host sending SSDP M-SEARCH to UDP 1900 LAN targets
              (C0XMO lateral spread from compromised device)

     Coverage notes:
       DD-WRT routers do not run Wazuh agents; no endpoint rules can
       fire on the router itself. Detection relies on:
         (a) Firewall/IDS observing the exploitation traffic on the LAN;
         (b) Any Linux pivot host that runs the C0XMO scanner generating
             detectable network and endpoint artifacts; and
         (c) Post-compromise: LAN hosts contacting the dropper server.
       If UPnP is disabled on all DD-WRT devices (recommended hardening),
       the attack surface is eliminated and these rules become monitoring
       for misconfiguration re-enablement.

     References:
       CISA KEV Catalog:
         https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog
       NVD CVE-2021-27137:
         https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2021-27137
       SSD Secure Disclosure - DD-WRT UPnP Buffer Overflow:
         https://ssd-disclosure.com/ssd-advisory-dd-wrt-upnp-buffer-overflow/
       FortiGuard IPS Signature 56117:
         https://www.fortiguard.com/encyclopedia/ips/56117
       FortiGuard Labs - C0XMO Gafgyt Variant Analysis:
         https://www.fortinet.com/blog/threat-research/inside-cross-platform-propagation-of-new-gafgyt-variant-c0xmo
       BleepingComputer - C0XMO Botnet Spreads via DD-WRT Flaw:
         https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/c0xmo-botnet-spreads-via-dd-wrt-router-flaw-kills-rival-malware/
       SOC Prime - C0XMO Gafgyt Variant:
         https://socprime.com/active-threats/c0xmo-a-new-gafgyt-variant-with-cross-platform-propagation/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1190:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1190/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1105:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1498:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1498/
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,impact,ddwrt,cve_2021_27137,cisa_kev,c0xmo,gafgyt,iot_botnet,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104128: Base anchor - inbound UDP 1900 from external IP
       Firewall log (firewall group)
       Level 0: no alert; chains to scanner rule 104129.

       UDP port 1900 is the SSDP/UPnP discovery port. DD-WRT with
       UPnP enabled listens on this port on internal interfaces.
       Inbound connections from external IPs are unexpected in a
       correctly configured network and indicate either a misconfigured
       firewall exposing UPnP to the internet, or a LAN-side attacker
       scanning for vulnerable routers. This anchor captures all
       external-sourced UDP 1900 activity for correlation by rule
       104129 (frequency-based scanner detection).
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104128" level="0">
    <if_group>firewall</if_group>
    <field name="data.dest_port">^1900$</field>
    <description>DD-WRT CVE-2021-27137: Inbound UDP 1900 (SSDP/UPnP) from $(data.src_ip) - base anchor for SSDP scanner detection (chains to 104129)</description>
    <group>ddwrt,cve_2021_27137,ssdp,upnp,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104129: SSDP M-SEARCH scanner - 5+ probes in 60 s
       Firewall log (firewall group), frequency rule on 104128.
       MITRE: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application)

       The C0XMO Python scanner probes LAN hosts in rapid succession
       to identify DD-WRT devices with UPnP enabled before delivering
       the CVE-2021-27137 exploit payload. Five or more SSDP probes
       from the same source IP within 60 seconds indicates automated
       UPnP reconnaissance, not a single device discovery event.
       In a correctly firewalled environment, external UDP 1900 should
       be blocked entirely; any such traffic reaching internal hosts
       warrants immediate investigation of the source IP and the
       DD-WRT devices it is targeting.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104129" level="11" frequency="5" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>104128</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>data.src_ip</same_field>
    <description>DD-WRT CVE-2021-27137: SSDP scanner  -  5+ UDP port 1900 probes from $(data.src_ip) within 60 s; automated UPnP reconnaissance consistent with C0XMO Gafgyt Python scanner probing for DD-WRT routers vulnerable to stack buffer overflow; block source IP and audit internal UPnP exposure (T1190)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ddwrt,cve_2021_27137,ssdp,upnp,scanner,automated_attack,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104130: IOC - Outbound to C0XMO dropper IP 217.160.125.125
       Firewall log (firewall group)
       MITRE: T1105 (Ingress Tool Transfer)

       217.160.125.125 (hosted in Germany) is the C0XMO dropper and
       scanner distribution server identified by FortiGuard Labs. It
       hosts the Python SSDP scanner and multi-architecture ELF botnet
       binaries (ARM, MIPS, MIPSEL, PowerPC, SuperH, MC68000, x86_64)
       served on port 15527 via HTTP. Any outbound connection from a
       LAN host to this IP strongly indicates that the CVE-2021-27137
       exploit shellcode ran on a DD-WRT router and the router is now
       fetching the C0XMO payload, OR that a compromised Linux pivot
       host is downloading the dropper package. Initiate incident
       response: isolate affected hosts and inspect for C0XMO processes
       and /tmp/.cache artifacts.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104130" level="14">
    <if_group>firewall</if_group>
    <field name="data.dest_ip">^217\.160\.125\.125$</field>
    <description>IOC: C0XMO Gafgyt dropper contact  -  outbound to 217.160.125.125 (Germany; CVE-2021-27137 DD-WRT exploit dropper; port 15527; multi-arch ELF payloads for ARM/MIPS/PowerPC); a DD-WRT router or LAN host is downloading the C0XMO botnet payload after successful exploit; isolate affected device and inspect for /tmp/.cache artifacts (T1105, T1496)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
      <id>T1496</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ioc_c2,ddwrt,cve_2021_27137,c0xmo,gafgyt,dropper,iot_botnet,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104131: Suricata - C0XMO dropper IP traffic
       Suricata EVE JSON (parent SID 86601)
       MITRE: T1105 (Ingress Tool Transfer)

       Suricata with LAN visibility will observe the TCP connection
       from the compromised DD-WRT router or pivot host to the C0XMO
       dropper at 217.160.125.125:15527. This rule fires on any
       Suricata alert involving that destination IP, covering both
       the initial HTTP dropper fetch and subsequent C2 check-ins.
       FortiGuard IPS signature 56117 (DD-WRT.UPNP.CVE-2021-27137.
       uuid.Buffer.Overflow) may also generate a Suricata-forwarded
       alert when the oversized ST:uuid: M-SEARCH packet is observed
       on the wire; this rule captures the dropper download phase
       that follows.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104131" level="14">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.dest_ip" type="pcre2">^217\.160\.125\.125$</field>
    <description>Suricata: C0XMO Gafgyt dropper traffic  -  connection to 217.160.125.125 (CVE-2021-27137 DD-WRT exploit dropper/scanner server; port 15527); network IDS confirms dropper fetch from compromised DD-WRT or Linux pivot host; inspect /tmp/.cache on all reachable LAN devices and block IOC IP at perimeter (T1105, T1496)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
      <id>T1496</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ioc_c2,ddwrt,cve_2021_27137,c0xmo,gafgyt,suricata,dropper,iot_botnet,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104132: Endpoint - wget/curl to C0XMO dropper port 15527
       Wazuh agent command monitoring / auditd (command group)
       MITRE: T1105 (Ingress Tool Transfer)

       The C0XMO shellcode executes wget or curl on the compromised
       DD-WRT router (which runs BusyBox) to fetch the architecture-
       appropriate ELF binary from 217.160.125.125:15527. The same
       command pattern is used by the Python scanner when deployed
       on a compromised Linux pivot host. An outbound wget or curl
       process targeting port 15527 is highly anomalous on any host
       in the environment; this port has no legitimate use case and
       is specific to C0XMO dropper delivery. This rule fires on
       Wazuh-monitored Linux endpoints that may serve as compromise
       pivots for the SSDP scanning campaign.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104132" level="12">
    <if_group>command</if_group>
    <match>wget|curl</match>
    <field name="data.command" type="pcre2">(?i)(wget|curl).{0,200}:15527\b</field>
    <description>DD-WRT CVE-2021-27137 / C0XMO: $(data.command)  -  wget or curl fetching from port 15527, the C0XMO Gafgyt dropper distribution port (217.160.125.125:15527); host is likely a Linux pivot used to deliver the DD-WRT exploit or has itself been compromised; inspect /tmp/.cache for ELF binaries and terminate suspicious processes (T1105)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ddwrt,cve_2021_27137,c0xmo,gafgyt,dropper,ingress_tool_transfer,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104133: Endpoint - /tmp/.cache ELF dropper staging
       Wazuh syscheck FIM (syscheck group, parent SID 554)
       MITRE: T1105 (Ingress Tool Transfer)

       C0XMO stages its multi-architecture ELF botnet binaries in
       /tmp/.cache on compromised devices before executing them. The
       .cache subdirectory within /tmp is the specific staging
       location observed across C0XMO samples (FortiGuard Labs 2026).
       File creation events under /tmp/.cache on any Wazuh-monitored
       Linux host indicate either: (a) the host itself was compromised
       by C0XMO exploitation and is staging its own botnet payload, or
       (b) the host received the C0XMO dropper as a result of the
       DD-WRT SSDP exploitation spreading to adjacent Linux systems.
       FIM monitoring of /tmp is recommended in syscheck configuration
       to ensure this rule fires on new file additions.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104133" level="11">
    <if_sid>554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)/tmp/\.cache/</field>
    <description>DD-WRT CVE-2021-27137 / C0XMO: New file created in /tmp/.cache [$(file)] on $(agent.name) - C0XMO Gafgyt botnet stages multi-architecture ELF payloads (ARM, MIPS, PowerPC) in /tmp/.cache after exploiting CVE-2021-27137 or other IoT vulnerabilities; inspect file type and hash, terminate any spawned processes, and check for BusyBox-stripped binaries consistent with cross-compiled IoT malware (T1105)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
      <id>T1496</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ddwrt,cve_2021_27137,c0xmo,gafgyt,dropper,syscheck,tmp_staging,iot_botnet,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104134: Firewall - Internal SSDP scanning (LAN propagation)
       Firewall log (firewall group)
       MITRE: T1584.005 (Compromise Infrastructure: Botnet)

       Once C0XMO infects a DD-WRT router or Linux host, it deploys
       an embedded Python scanner that probes the local LAN for
       additional UPnP-enabled devices vulnerable to CVE-2021-27137
       and other IoT exploits. Internal SSDP scanning — where a LAN
       host sends UDP M-SEARCH to multiple other internal IPs on
       port 1900 — is abnormal behavior for most homelab devices.
       Routers and IoT devices should not initiate M-SEARCH probes
       across subnets; only UPnP control point software (e.g. media
       servers) would legitimately query port 1900, and only within
       a single subnet. Cross-subnet or high-frequency internal UDP
       1900 traffic indicates a compromised device running the C0XMO
       scanner for lateral propagation within the network.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104134" level="12">
    <if_group>firewall</if_group>
    <field name="data.dest_port">^1900$</field>
    <field name="data.src_ip" type="pcre2">^(10\.|172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\.|192\.168\.)</field>
    <field name="data.dest_ip" type="pcre2">^(10\.|172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\.|192\.168\.)</field>
    <description>DD-WRT CVE-2021-27137 / C0XMO: Internal host $(data.src_ip) sending SSDP M-SEARCH to $(data.dest_ip):1900  -  LAN-to-LAN UDP 1900 scanning is the C0XMO Gafgyt propagation pattern; compromised DD-WRT router or Linux host is running the embedded Python SSDP scanner to recruit additional vulnerable UPnP devices; isolate source host and audit all internal DD-WRT devices for UPnP exposure (T1584.005, T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1584.005</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ddwrt,cve_2021_27137,c0xmo,gafgyt,ssdp,upnp,lateral_spread,iot_botnet,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
